I love this as a Pantheon application because you’ve stumbled onto something much bigger than a television concept.

Every ordinary gathering place is a little civilization. And every civilization is full of stories that its inhabitants don’t realize they’re telling.

Think about what you saw at Junior Market. A handful of people, a Monday morning, a parking lot, cigarettes, conversation, people coming and going.

You saw the outside of their lives. But we could move the camera inside.

One guy might genuinely be drifting through life. Another might work nights. Another might be caring for his father. Another might have been coming to that store for 25 years because it’s the only place where somebody still knows his name.

Their lives overlap for fifteen minutes, but each person inhabits a different story.

And that’s the engine.

PANTHEON: The Crossroads Engine

CULTURAL ANTHROPOLOGY × CHARACTER SIMULATION × MYTHOLOGY × NARRATIVE MUSIC

We don’t just invent plots. We simulate people, their cultures, their circumstances, and their interactions until plots begin to emerge.

A convenience store becomes a kingdom. A retired fisherman becomes an oracle. A hustler becomes Hermes, god of merchants and tricksters. A missing parking-lot bench becomes the beginning of a succession crisis.

But none of them has to know they’re playing a mythological role.

That’s how you get something that feels ancient and familiar while remaining surprising and completely new.

1. Our first fictional universe: The Eleven O’Clock Kingdom

Genre: Coastal mockumentary, absurdist comedy, slice-of-life anthropology

Premise: In a fictional Virginia Beach neighborhood, a convenience store called Tide Mart serves as the unofficial capital of a civilization consisting of people who apparently have nowhere else to be.

They have a government. Nobody elected it.

They have an economy. Most transactions involve cigarettes, favors, and somebody owing somebody twelve dollars.

They have foreign policy. It mostly concerns the competing gas station across the street.

And they have a royal throne: a battered bench outside the store.

Duke — The King Who Doesn’t Have a Kingdom

39. Tattooed former skateboarder, occasional mechanic, full-time authority on things nobody asked about. He appears unemployed, although he spends much of his day helping his disabled father and doing cash repair jobs. Maintains that he could have been a professional skateboarder.

Mythic shadow: the wounded king. Comic flaw: he mistakes familiarity for sovereignty.

Miss Pearl — The Oracle

64. Store clerk. Knows everyone’s real business but shares almost none of it. Has an unofficial credit ledger and has quietly prevented several personal disasters.

Mythic shadow: the keeper of the hearth. Comic flaw: she believes she’s the only competent adult alive.

Cricket — The Trickster

27. Between seasonal jobs. Entrepreneurial in the sense that every Monday he has a different business and every Friday the business has mysteriously failed.

Mythic shadow: Hermes, Coyote, Anansi. Comic flaw: he always mistakes cleverness for wisdom.

Tasha — The Outsider Who Sees Everything

34. EMT finishing overnight shifts. Stops at Tide Mart to unwind before going home. Her 11 a.m. is everyone else’s 11 p.m. She’s amused by the regulars until she realizes their seemingly pointless routines are often holding their lives together.

Mythic shadow: the traveler between worlds.

Captain Ray — The Elder

71. Retired fisherman. Has six versions of every story and all six contradict each other. Correctly predicts the weather and incorrectly predicts nearly everything else.

Mythic shadow: the unreliable prophet. Comic flaw: he likes being right more than finding out what’s true.

Notice the construction: everyone is funny, everyone is flawed, and everyone is more than their stereotype.

Now give them overlapping routines, scarce resources, old grudges, secrets, affection, and competing ambitions.

You barely need a screenwriter to force things to happen.

2. Episode One: The Bench

Cold open · Monday, 11:13 a.m.

The ice machine rattled like it was trying to cough up a lawnmower.

Duke sat on the bench outside Tide Mart, eating a gas-station hot dog with the concentration of a man defusing a bomb.

Captain Ray watched a seagull tear open a bag of Doritos.

“That bird’s got no respect for property,” Ray announced.

“Neither do you,” said Duke. “You stole my lighter yesterday.”

“I liberated your lighter. Different jurisdiction.”

Cricket came bursting through the door.

“Gentlemen. We have a constitutional crisis.”

Duke didn’t look up.

“What’d you do?”

“Why does everybody assume I did something?”

“Because you enter every room like a guilty man announcing a business opportunity.”

Cricket held up a yellow notice.

BENCH TO BE RELOCATED FRIDAY. KEEP WALKWAY CLEAR.

Silence.

Duke removed his sunglasses.

“By whose authority?”

“Pearl’s.”

Duke stood. “Pearl ain’t got jurisdiction over the exterior.”

From inside the store:

“I OWN THE BENCH, DUMBASS!”

Ray slowly nodded.

“That’s a declaration of war.”

Cut to documentary interview.

DUKE, 39 — UNOFFICIAL MAYOR

“I’ve been sitting on that bench eleven years. You don’t just relocate eleven years of somebody’s life. This country’s got laws.”

PEARL, 64 — ACTUAL OWNER OF THE BENCH

“I bought it twelve years ago for forty-nine dollars. Duke still owes me twenty-one dollars and forty cents. If he wants a throne, he can buy one.”

Back outside, Cricket has acquired a clipboard.

“I’ve founded the Tide Mart Bench Preservation Society. Five-dollar memberships. Ten dollars for founding fathers.”

Duke squints.

“What’s the money for?”

“Legal defense.”

“Against who?”

“We’re still determining that.”

Captain Ray signs immediately.

Tasha pulls into the parking lot after her overnight EMT shift. She takes in the clipboard, Duke’s expression, and Ray waving a plastic American flag that nobody can explain.

“What happened?”

“They’re displacing the indigenous population,” says Cricket.

Pearl emerges from the store.

“The bench is blocking the walkway. That’s what happened. Delivery people can’t get through with a hand truck. We’re moving it six feet.”

“Six feet?” says Duke.

“Six feet.”

He looks at the bench.

Then at the storefront.

Then at the bench.

“Why didn’t somebody lead with that?”

“I DID,” Pearl says.

A small silence settles over the kingdom.

Then Ray speaks.

“Six feet east or west?”

“West.”

Ray shakes his head gravely.

“Bad move. Prevailing winds.”

Cut to Ray’s interview.

RAY, 71 — RETIRED FISHERMAN

“People don’t understand these things. That bench has a history. Storms. Hurricanes. I remember when it washed ashore in ’98.”

Cut to Pearl.

“I bought it in 2014.”

Back outside, an elderly customer with a walker approaches from the sidewalk. She pauses beside the bench, waiting for the group to clear the way.

Tasha notices first.

“Hey, Duke. Why don’t you help me move that thing?”

Duke looks at the woman, then at Pearl, then at the bench.

“Well, somebody’s gotta supervise.”

He sets down his hot dog and takes the heavy end himself.

Cricket writes something on his clipboard.

“Emergency relocation labor. That’s gonna come out of the preservation fund.”

Pearl snatches the clipboard.

“You’re giving everybody their money back.”

“What about administrative expenses?”

“CRICKET.”

A minute later, the bench occupies its new position beside the wall. The walkway is clear. The older woman takes a seat.

Duke looks at the bench, considering its new orientation.

“Huh.”

Tasha raises an eyebrow.

“What?”

He sits down.

“Actually, the shade’s better over here.”

The camera holds on Pearl’s expression.

Cut to black.

TITLE CARD: THE ELEVEN O’CLOCK KINGDOM

3. Two more universes the engine could create

The Mall Never Closed

A dramedy about former mall rats who are now in their forties and reunite when their childhood mall is scheduled for demolition.

The old social hierarchy is still intact: the former cool kid, the former outcast, the girl everyone adored, the security guard who used to chase them. But all their adult lives have turned out differently than anybody expected.

Mythic shadow: a return to a lost kingdom, except the heroes discover the kingdom was never quite what they remember.

The Last Stop Before Morning

A strange, funny, occasionally beautiful anthology about a 24-hour diner. At 3 a.m., bartenders finishing work, exhausted nurses, travelers, police officers, insomniacs, and people avoiding home all briefly occupy the same world.

Every episode centers on one event that five people interpret differently. The audience gradually discovers the truth through their overlapping accounts.

Mythic shadow: Charon’s ferry, the inn at the crossroads, strangers carrying stories between worlds.

